<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n31677" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ob-līdo</span></b> , si, sum, 3,
			v. a.
		laedo. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>To squeeze together</i> (rare but class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">caelum digitulis duobus oblidere, Cic. Scaur.
					§ 10, p. 261 Orell.: oblisis faucibus,</span>”
				<i>strangled</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Tac. A.
					5, 9</span></i>
			; so, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">obliso gutture,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Prud. Psych. 589</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">App. M. 4, p.
			147</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>To squeeze</i> or <i>crush to
			pieces</i> (post-Aug.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">fetus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Col.
					7, 3, 8</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">oblisus pondere,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin. Ep. 6, 20, 16</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
